{"joy":{"id":"986dc890-c5d5-40ca-811c-acc8a77d43f8","word":"joy","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"feeling of happiness","tags":["feminine"],"word":"alegria"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"a child's joy on Christmas morning","bold_text_offsets":[[10,13]],"type":"example","id":"177cd822-fa39-4b6c-8b63-61d225545e1a"},{"text":"It is his joy to walk in the rain.","bold_text_offsets":[[10,13]],"type":"example","id":"1afbe246-e56c-45a3-b5fb-12adba88c47f"},{"text":"[…]purſued his vnneighbourly purpoſe in ſuch ſort: that hee being the ſtronger perſwader, and ſhe (belike) too credulous in beleeuing or elſe ouer-feeble in reſiſting, from priuate imparlance, they fell to action; and continued their cloſe fight a long while together, vnſeene and vvithout ſuſpition, no doubt to their equall ioy and contentment.","ref":"1620, Giovanni Bocaccio, translated by John Florio, The Decameron, Containing an Hundred Pleaſant Nouels: Wittily Diſcourſed, Betweene Seuen Honourable Ladies, and Three Noble Gentlemen, Isaac Iaggard, Nouell 8, The Eighth Day:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[326,329]],"id":"a5f6b605-555f-4d00-8d0b-1bd8a955bf05"},{"text":"It was a joy to snatch some brief respite, and find himself in the rectory drawing–room. Listening here was as pleasant as talking; just to watch was pleasant. The young priests who lived here wore cassocks and birettas; their faces were fine and mild, yet really strong, like the rector's face; and in their intercourse with him and his wife they seemed to be brothers.","ref":"1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter X, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[9,12]],"id":"54755de0-217a-49cb-940d-9231ba090446"}],"links":[["happiness","happiness"],["cheerfulness","cheerfulness"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"elation"},{"word":"glee"},{"word":"blitheness","source":"Thesaurus:happiness"},{"word":"blith